    "headline": "Oku is rooted in Old Japanese despite overlapping with a goon reading from Middle Chinese, and is cognate with 沖 (oki, “the open sea, offshore”).",
    "morphemes": [],
    "sources": [
      {
        "quote": "Despite the apparent overlap with the modern goon reading of oku that is derived from Middle Chinese, this term is actually rooted in Old Japanese, appearing in the Man'yōshū collection of poetry dating to roughly 759 CE. Cognate with 沖 (oki, “the open sea, offshore”), originally in reference to “somewhere far removed, possibly out of sight”. While oki came to mean “far removed in the wide open → far off shore”, oku instead came to mean “far removed inside → inside, interior”."
      }
    ],
